artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a spatial single-cell dataset in which, for each cell $i$ in a population $\{1, \dots, N\}$, one observes (a) a gene expression vector $g_i \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}^{G}$ giving counts of $G$ transcript species, and (b) a high-resolution image $I_i$ (or segmented mask) capturing the cell's morphology. Gene expression admits a canonical tabular encoding as the count matrix $X \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}^{N \times G}$, but morphology has no analogous universal tabular representation. Two broad classes of morphology encodings are available:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Engineered feature maps $\phi_{\mathrm{eng}}: I_i \mapsto m_i \in \mathbb{R}^{d}$ producing interpretable handcrafted descriptors (e.g. area, perimeter, eccentricity, intensity moments, texture features).
\item Learned embeddings $\phi_{\theta}: I_i \mapsto z_i \in \mathbb{R}^{k}$ obtained from a neural network trained with a self-supervised, supervised, or multimodal objective.
\end{enumerate}
Determine a representation $\phi$ of cellular morphology that is optimal for joint statistical analysis with the gene expression count matrix $X$. Specifically:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Formulate criteria (e.g. preservation of biologically meaningful variation, alignment with transcriptomic structure, invariance to nuisance imaging factors, identifiability, interpretability) under which optimality of $\phi$ can be defined.
\item Decide whether a fixed engineered $\phi_{\mathrm{eng}}$, a learned $\phi_{\theta}$, or a hybrid construction best satisfies these criteria.
\item Provide a construction of $\phi$ together with a joint model $p(g_i, \phi(I_i))$ enabling integrated downstream tasks such as cell-type identification, spatial domain inference, and morphology--expression association testing.
\end{enumerate}

